A subclass gets all the parent's methods, can override them, and can call the parent's version with `super()`:

```python
class Savings(Account):
    def __init__(self, owner, rate):
        super().__init__(owner)
        self.rate = rate

    def add_interest(self):
        return self.deposit(self.balance * self.rate)
```

You'll meet inheritance mostly when frameworks ask you to subclass their base classes. In your own code, small classes and plain functions usually beat deep hierarchies.

